<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for Patina.app. macos-sign.sh applies them to
  the app, that is to Contents/MacOS/patina. On a library they would mean
  nothing, so the dylibs in Contents/Frameworks are signed without them.

  Every entitlement switches off part of the hardened runtime, so this lists
  only what Patina cannot run without, which is one.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

    For LuaJIT, which runs mpv's Lua scripts. Patina refuses a libmpv without
    Lua (mpv's yt-dlp hook, which plays online video, is a Lua script, and so
    are the scripts mpv loads by default and any a user adds), and the one in
    the bundle uses LuaJIT. LuaJIT's compiler writes machine code into memory
    it mapped itself and then makes that memory executable. The hardened
    runtime kills a process that runs such memory ("Code Signature Invalid",
    "Invalid Page") unless it has this entitlement, or the memory was mapped
    with MAP_JIT and the process has com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit.

    allow-jit is the narrower of the two and would be the one to use, but only
    a LuaJIT built with LUAJIT_ENABLE_OSX_HRT maps its code with MAP_JIT (see
    src/lj_mcode.c and src/lj_ccallback.c in LuaJIT 2.1: commit 4f2bb19 put the
    MAP_JIT path behind that option, issue 1334). Built without it, LuaJIT maps
    ordinary anonymous memory and mprotect()s it executable, and allow-jit on
    its own does not prevent the kill; issue 1334 opens with exactly that
    crash. The libluajit-5.1.2.dylib that macos-libmpv.txt pins is built
    without the option: neither slice imports pthread_jit_write_protect_np,
    and every mmap() in it passes MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON (0x1002) and no MAP_JIT
    (0x800). IINA, whose build it is, ships this entitlement for the same
    reason (IINA commit a4ae19d replaced allow-jit with it, issue 3551).

    macos-sign.sh checks the LuaJIT it is about to sign against this file and
    stops if the two disagree. A refreshed macos-libmpv.txt that brings a
    LuaJIT built with LUAJIT_ENABLE_OSX_HRT is the time to replace this
    entitlement with allow-jit.

    (FFmpeg's libswscale also makes generated code executable, on x86_64 and
    only for its fast-bilinear scaler, which mpv uses only when asked to.)

  Left out on purpose:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: nothing in the bundle maps MAP_JIT.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: every library in
    Contents/Frameworks is signed with the same Developer ID as the app, which
    is all library validation asks. What it refuses is code from elsewhere: a
    libmpv named by PATINA_LIBMPV or found in Homebrew's or MacPorts' prefix,
    mpv C plugins, Lua C modules, libbluray's libaacs. Patina carries its own
    libmpv and finds it even when started through a link, such as the
    Homebrew cask's patina command (src/mpv/ffi.rs resolves the link first),
    so it needs none of those.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: nothing relies on
    DYLD_ variables.

    Sandbox, network, file and Apple-event entitlements: Patina is not
    sandboxed, so it needs none to reach the network or the files it is
    given. It receives Apple events (Finder's opens, patina:// links) and
    sends none, and its OpenSubtitles password is kept in the login keychain,
    which needs no entitlement either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
